-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/141a06c9-023c-42fd-80ec-8d80bacdfcfe
-- title:
--   Lemma 5 — $B(X^*,X_{t+1})\le B(X^*,X_t)+2\varepsilon_t$
-- statement:
--   Let $X^*$ be optimal with Assumption 1 for $\delta>0$, and let $\{X_t\}$ be a run of Algorithm 2 with $r^*=\operatorname{rank}(X^*)\le r<n$, with $\eta_t\le1/\beta$ and $\varepsilon_t\in(0,3/4]$ for $t\ge1$, $\varepsilon_0>0$, and $\lambda_{r^*+1}(X_t)\le\varepsilon_{t-1}/(n-r)$ for all $t\ge1$. Let $G\ge\sup_{\mathcal S_n}\|\nabla f\|_2$. Then for any $t\ge1$, if $X_t$ satisfies (19) with $\xi=0$, $\varepsilon_-=\varepsilon_{t-1}$, $\varepsilon=\varepsilon_t$ and $\eta=\eta_t$, it holds that
--   $$
--   B(X^*,X_{t+1})\le B(X^*,X_t)+2\varepsilon_t.
--   $$
--   So the iterates can leave the ball around $X^*$ only at the summable rate of $\varepsilon_t$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: G = sup over 𝒮_n of ‖∇f‖₂ → G any upper bound on it; reason: the page's G is one such bound, and Theorem 5 invokes the lemma with G ≥ sup
--   - encoding: the sequence {X_t} generated by Algorithm 2 → any X : ℕ → Matrix with IsRun, where each step may choose any valid top-r eigenvectors; reason: the page leaves the choice of eigenvectors open at ties, so every choice is covered
--   - encoding: step size up to 1/β → 0 < β; reason: excludes an undefined case only
--   - encoding: ε_t ∈ (0, 3/4] for t ≥ 1, with ε₀ ∈ [0, 1] from Algorithm 2 → 0 < ε₀ in addition; reason: at ε₀ = 0 the iterate X₁ can be singular and log(X₁) undefined; excludes an undefined case only
--   - encoding: the gradient ∇f → a map grad tied to f by HasFDerivAt at symmetric matrices (Setting.hasGrad); reason: Lean needs ∇f as data; same instances
--   - encoding: λ_i with 1-based index → lam X i, with value 0 outside 1 ≤ i ≤ n; reason: every index used is in range under the hypotheses
-- source:
--   Garber & Kaplan, On the Efficient Implementation of the Matrix Exponentiated Gradient Algorithm for Low-Rank Matrix Optimization, arXiv:2012.10469v2, Lemma 5, p.19

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LowRankMEG_Setting
import Definitions.Def_LowRankMEG_IsOptimal
import Definitions.Def_LowRankMEG_StrictComplementarity
import Definitions.Def_LowRankMEG_IsRun
import Definitions.Def_PSDPolyApprox_densityMatrices
import Definitions.Def_LowRankMEG_bregman
import Definitions.Def_LowRankMEG_lam
import Definitions.Def_LowRankMEG_Cond19
import Definitions.Def_WainwrightHDS_NucNorm_opNorm2

namespace LowRankMEG

/-- **Lemma 5**: along a run with `η_t ≤ 1/β`, `ε_t ∈ (0, 3/4]` and `λ_{r*+1}(X_t) ≤ ε_{t−1}/(n − r)` for all `t ≥ 1`:
for any `t ≥ 1`, if `X_t` satisfies (19) with `ξ = 0`, then `B(X*, X_{t+1}) ≤ B(X*, X_t) + 2ε_t`.
Garber & Kaplan, *On the Efficient Implementation of the Matrix Exponentiated Gradient Algorithm for
Low-Rank Matrix Optimization*, arXiv:2012.10469v2, Lemma 5, p.19.

**Formalization Note.** (19) at step `t` has `ε₋ = ε_{t−1}`, `ε = ε_t`, `η = η_t`. `t - 1` is exact, as `t ≥ 1`.
`0 < ε₀`, because `X₁` must be positive definite for `log(X₁)` to exist. `G` is any upper bound on `‖∇f‖₂`
over `𝒮_n`. -/
theorem lemma_5 {n : ℕ} {f : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ → ℝ}
    {grad : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ} {β : ℝ}
    (hS : Setting f grad β) (hβ : 0 < β)
    {Xs : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ} {δ G : ℝ} {r : ℕ} {η ε : ℕ → ℝ} {X : ℕ → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ}
    (hopt : IsOptimal f Xs) (hsc : StrictComplementarity grad Xs δ)
    (hrun : IsRun grad η ε r X) (hrs : Xs.rank ≤ r)
    (hηβ : ∀ t, 1 ≤ t → η t ≤ 1 / β)
    (hε0 : 0 < ε 0) (hε : ∀ t, 1 ≤ t → ε t ∈ Set.Ioc (0 : ℝ) (3 / 4))
    (hlam : ∀ t, 1 ≤ t → lam (X t) (Xs.rank + 1) ≤ ε (t - 1) / ((n : ℝ) - r))
    (hG : ∀ W ∈ PSDPolyApprox.densityMatrices n, WainwrightHDS.NucNorm.opNorm2 (grad W) ≤ G)
    (t : ℕ) (ht : 1 ≤ t) (h19 : Cond19 β G δ Xs (η t) (ε (t - 1)) (ε t) 0 (X t)) :
    bregman Xs (X (t + 1)) ≤ bregman Xs (X t) + 2 * ε t := by sorry

end LowRankMEG
